Lab 10: Install and Configure RHACS for Multicluster Security
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

• Install Red Hat Advanced Cluster Security (RHACS) on OpenShift clusters • Configure comprehensive security policies for multicluster environments • Implement runtime protection mechanisms across multiple clusters • Perform vulnerability scanning and compliance assessments • Monitor and respond to security threats in real-time • Understand the architecture and components of RHACS • Configure network policies and admission controllers • Generate security reports and compliance dashboards

Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

• Basic understanding of Kubernetes and OpenShift concepts • Familiarity with container security principles • Knowledge of YAML configuration files • Experience with command-line interfaces • Understanding of network security concepts • Basic knowledge of compliance frameworks (CIS, NIST, PCI-DSS)

Technical Requirements: • OpenShift cluster with cluster-admin privileges • Minimum 16GB RAM and 4 CPU cores available • Internet connectivity for downloading RHACS components • kubectl and oc CLI tools installed

Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines: Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines with OpenShift clusters already set up. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment - no need to build your own VM or install OpenShift from scratch.

Task 1: Install RHACS on OpenShift
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the Environment
First, let's verify our OpenShift cluster is ready and create the necessary namespace for RHACS.

Step 1: Verify cluster access and status

# Check cluster status
oc cluster-info

# Verify you have cluster-admin privileges
oc auth can-i '*' '*' --all-namespaces

# Check available nodes
oc get nodes
Step 2: Create the RHACS namespace

# Create the stackrox namespace for RHACS
oc create namespace stackrox

# Verify namespace creation
oc get namespaces | grep stackrox
Step 3: Set up environment variables

# Set environment variables for easier management
export RHACS_NAMESPACE="stackrox"
export CLUSTER_NAME="primary-cluster"
export RHACS_VERSION="4.4.2"

# Verify variables are set
echo "RHACS Namespace: $RHACS_NAMESPACE"
echo "Cluster Name: $CLUSTER_NAME"
echo "RHACS Version: $RHACS_VERSION"
Subtask 1.2: Install RHACS Operator
Step 1: Create the OperatorGroup for RHACS

# Create rhacs-operator-group.yaml
cat << EOF > rhacs-operator-group.yaml
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1
kind: OperatorGroup
metadata:
  name: rhacs-operator-group
  namespace: stackrox
spec:
  targetNamespaces:
  - stackrox
EOF
Step 2: Apply the OperatorGroup

# Apply the operator group
oc apply -f rhacs-operator-group.yaml

# Verify operator group creation
oc get operatorgroup -n stackrox
Step 3: Create the Subscription for RHACS Operator

# Create rhacs-subscription.yaml
cat << EOF > rhacs-subscription.yaml
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: rhacs-operator
  namespace: stackrox
spec:
  channel: stable
  name: rhacs-operator
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
EOF
Step 4: Apply the subscription

# Apply the subscription
oc apply -f rhacs-subscription.yaml

# Wait for operator installation (this may take 2-3 minutes)
oc get csv -n stackrox -w
Step 5: Verify operator installation

# Check if the operator is running
oc get pods -n stackrox

# Verify the ClusterServiceVersion
oc get csv -n stackrox | grep rhacs
Subtask 1.3: Deploy RHACS Central
Step 1: Create the Central custom resource

# Create rhacs-central.yaml
cat << EOF > rhacs-central.yaml
apiVersion: platform.stackrox.io/v1alpha1
kind: Central
metadata:
  name: stackrox-central-services
  namespace: stackrox
spec:
  central:
    exposure:
      loadBalancer:
        enabled: false
        port: 443
      nodePort:
        enabled: false
      route:
        enabled: true
    persistence:
      persistentVolumeClaim:
        claimName: stackrox-db
    resources:
      requests:
        memory: "4Gi"
        cpu: "1500m"
      limits:
        memory: "8Gi"
        cpu: "4000m"
  egress:
    connectivityPolicy: Online
  scanner:
    analyzer:
      scaling:
        autoScaling: Enabled
        maxReplicas: 5
        minReplicas: 2
        replicas: 3
    scannerComponent: Enabled
EOF
Step 2: Deploy RHACS Central

# Apply the Central configuration
oc apply -f rhacs-central.yaml

# Monitor the deployment progress
oc get pods -n stackrox -w
Step 3: Wait for Central to be ready

# Check Central status (wait until all pods are running)
oc get central -n stackrox

# Verify all components are running
oc get pods -n stackrox | grep -E "(central|scanner)"
Step 4: Get the Central route

# Get the Central UI route
CENTRAL_ROUTE=$(oc get route central -n stackrox -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "RHACS Central UI: https://$CENTRAL_ROUTE"

# Save the route for later use
echo "export CENTRAL_ROUTE=$CENTRAL_ROUTE" >> ~/.bashrc
